---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const oneStepSnippet = `npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents`;

const clientsSnippet = `npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents                   # Claude Code: .claude/skills + .mcp.json
npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents --client cursor   # Cursor: .cursor/mcp.json
npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents --client vscode   # VS Code: .vscode/mcp.json
npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents --client codex    # Codex: .codex/config.toml
npx @kernelui-lib/cli agents --dir .agents/skills --no-mcp   # skills only, anywhere`;

const manualMcpSnippet = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "kernel": { "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@kernelui-lib/cli", "mcp"] }
  }
}`;

const codexSnippet = `# .codex/config.toml (or ~/.codex/config.toml)
[mcp_servers.kernel]
command = "npx"
args = ["-y", "@kernelui-lib/cli", "mcp"]`;

const promptSnippet = `Build this UI with Kernel UI (@kernelui-lib/react). Before writing markup,
look up components and their exact props with the kernel MCP server (or
npx @kernelui-lib/cli docs <name> --markdown). Style only with --kernel-*
tokens. When you're done, run npx @kernelui-lib/cli check on the files you
changed and fix what it reports.`;

const checkSnippet = `npx @kernelui-lib/cli check src/**/*.tsx`;

const eslintSnippet = `// eslint.config.js
import kernel from "@kernelui-lib/eslint-plugin";

export default [kernel.configs.recommended];`;
---

<BaseLayout title="AI agents" description="Set up Claude Code, Cursor, VS Code or any coding agent to build with Kernel: one command installs skills, an MCP server with exact props, and a code check." withSidebar>
  <DocsSidebar slot="sidebar" />
  <div class="docs-prose">
    <h1>AI agents</h1>
    <p>
      Kernel is built to be used by coding agents as well as people. Every
      component's exact API is generated from its source, the conventions
      are the same across all of them, and there's a checker that tells an
      agent what it got wrong and how to fix it. You don't need to paste
      docs into a prompt.
    </p>

    <h2>Set up a project in one step</h2>
    <CodeBlock code={oneStepSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <p>This does three things, each of which you can also do on its own:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Installs Kernel's skills</strong> into
        <code>.claude/skills</code>. They ship inside
        <code>@kernelui-lib/react</code> and <code>@kernelui-lib/elements</code>,
        so they always match the version you installed:
        <code>kernel-ui</code> (building), <code>kernel-ui-theming</code>,
        <code>kernel-ui-migrate</code> (from shadcn/ui or Radix) and
        <code>kernel-ui-elements</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Registers the Kernel MCP server</strong>, so the agent can
        look components up instead of guessing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Adds a short Kernel section to <code>AGENTS.md</code></strong>
        (and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> if you have one). Codex, Cursor, Copilot
        and most other agents read <code>AGENTS.md</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <CodeBlock code={clientsSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2>The MCP server</h2>
    <p>
      <code>npx @kernelui-lib/cli mcp</code> runs a Model Context Protocol
      server over stdio. It answers from data bundled into the CLI, so it
      works offline and matches the version you installed. To add it to any
      MCP client by hand:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={manualMcpSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <p>Codex reads TOML instead:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={codexSnippet} lang="bash" />
    <div class="docs-table-scroll"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Tools the Kernel MCP server provides.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Tool</th><th scope="col">What it gives the agent</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>list_components</code></td><td>Every component, with its category and summary</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>search_components</code></td><td>The right component for a need in plain words, like "date range" or "tool approval"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_component</code></td><td>Exact React props (types, defaults, notes), custom element attributes and events, why the component uses its native element, and migration notes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_tokens</code></td><td>Design tokens with their values and what each is for</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get_conventions</code></td><td>Install steps and the conventions every component shares</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>check_code</code></td><td>A review of code the agent just wrote, with each fix spelled out</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>Let the agent check its own work</h2>
    <p>
      <code>kernel check</code> (and the MCP <code>check_code</code> tool)
      catches the mistakes agents make most often, and says how to fix each
      one: props from other libraries (<code>asChild</code>,
      <code>leftIcon</code>), invalid values, fields without a label,
      icon-only buttons without a name, clickable <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s,
      raw colours and hand-picked radii. It exits with an error when it finds
      one, so it also works in CI.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={checkSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2 id="lint">Lint rules</h2>
    <p>
      For the same checks in your editor and CI on every save, add the ESLint
      plugin. It recognises Kernel components by their import, and every
      message says how to fix the problem.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock code={eslintSnippet} lang="tsx" />
    <div class="docs-table-scroll"><table class="docs-table">
      <caption>Rules in kernel.configs.recommended.</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Catches</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr id="lint-no-foreign-props"><td><code>no-foreign-props</code> (error)</td><td>Props from other libraries: <code>asChild</code>, <code>leftIcon</code>, <code>isDisabled</code>…, with Kernel's equivalent</td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-valid-prop-values"><td><code>valid-prop-values</code> (error)</td><td>Values a prop doesn't accept, such as <code>variant="primry"</code></td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-field-requires-label"><td><code>field-requires-label</code> (error)</td><td>Form fields without <code>label</code></td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-icon-button-requires-label"><td><code>icon-button-requires-label</code> (error)</td><td>Icon-only Buttons without <code>aria-label</code></td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-no-clickable-non-interactive"><td><code>no-clickable-non-interactive</code> (error)</td><td>Click handlers on <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>s and other non-controls</td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-no-unknown-props"><td><code>no-unknown-props</code> (warn)</td><td>Props a component doesn't have, with the closest real one</td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-no-raw-colors"><td><code>no-raw-colors</code> (warn)</td><td>Raw colours in inline styles instead of tokens</td></tr>
        <tr id="lint-prefer-kernel-components"><td><code>prefer-kernel-components</code> (warn)</td><td>shadcn/ui and Radix imports Kernel replaces</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>

    <h2>A prompt that works</h2>
    <p>If your agent doesn't have the skills or MCP server yet, start with this:</p>
    <CodeBlock code={promptSnippet} lang="bash" />

    <h2>Machine-readable docs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a>: what Kernel is, when it fits, and an index of every page</li>
      <li><a href="/llms-full.txt">/llms-full.txt</a>: every guide and component page in one file</li>
      <li>Any page as markdown: add <code>.md</code> to its URL, for example <a href="/components/button.md">/components/button.md</a></li>
      <li><a href="/api.json">/api.json</a>: every component's exact props, attributes and events, generated from source</li>
      <li><a href="/registry.json">/registry.json</a>: the component catalogue, with shadcn aliases and migration notes</li>
      <li><code>llms.txt</code> and <code>skills/</code> inside each npm package, versioned with the code</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
